  "id": "DrugBank_DB01364 - MONDO_5709",
  "verbalization": "Ephedrine is implicated in the pathogenesis of the common cold through a series of interconnected pathways, primarily involving nasopharyngitis. The evidence suggests that Ephedrine may act as a precursor in the development of nasopharyngitis, which in turn is linked to the common cold. This relationship is supported by the presence of multiple indirect pathways, where Ephedrine is associated with nasopharyngitis, and nasopharyngitis is associated with the common cold. The shared indication of nasopharyngitis across different pathways indicates a potential role in the inflammatory cascade that contributes to the common cold.\n\nThe mechanisms underlying this relationship are not fully elucidated, but the presence of multiple indications and the cumulative effect of the pathways suggest a possible involvement of inflammatory mediators or immune responses. The integration of these pathways implies that Ephedrine may influence the inflammatory process, potentially leading to the development of nasopharyngitis and subsequent common cold symptoms. While the exact mechanism remains to be determined, the evidence supports a plausible mechanistic link between Ephedrine and the common cold through the involvement of nasopharyngitis.",
